WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.680
The log of 'a' to the base 'b' is equal to 'x'.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.700
How do we write this in the exponential form?

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.640
We can write it as 'b' raised to 'x' is equal to 'a'.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.840
Both the equations tell us the same thing.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.600
Now before we move forward

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.760
there are a few fundamental properties we need to know.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.920
Let's discuss the first one.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.900
What will log of 1 to the base 'b' be equal to?

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.340
How do we find this answer?

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.480
I encourage you to pause the video,

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.660
and find the answer to this.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.080
Say it equals 'x'.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.500
In the exponential form,

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.620
it can be written as 'b' raised to 'x' is equal to 1.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:49.700
Remember,

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.320
the exponential form is what most of us are comfortable with.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.940
This can help us find the value of 'x'.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.280
For the answer to be 1, the power here has to be 0.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.080
Do you remember that property of exponents?

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.160
Any non zero number raised to 0 is equal to 1.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.460
Hence, 'x' here is 0.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.660
The log of 1 to any base will always equal 0.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.960
Log of 1 to the base 12 is equal to 0.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.400
Log of 1 to the base 250 is also equal to 0.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.080
That was the first fundamental property we looked at.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.360
Log of 1 to any base will always equal 0.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.820
Now let's move on to the second property.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.860
What will be the value of log of 'b' to the base 'b'?

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.720
Give it a shot.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.460
Assume that it equals 'x'.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.440
So in the exponential form,

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.140
it can be written as 'b' raised to 'x' is equal to 'b'.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.780
What will be the value of 'x' here?

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.800
Come on, it's easy!

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.500
'b' to the power 1 will equal 'b'.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.760
'x' will be equal to 1.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.520
The log of 'b' to the base 'b' will equal 1.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.140
If the argument and the base are the same,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.580
then the logarithm will be 1.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.360
For instance, the log of 4 to the base 4 will be equal to 1!

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.800
That was the second property.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.980
The third one is pretty cool.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:23.100
It talks about the log of 'b' raised to 'n' to the base 'b'.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.300
Assuming this equals 'x'

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.200
try writing this in the exponential form.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.540
It is written as 'b' raised to 'x'

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.900
is equal to 'b' raised to 'n'.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.260
It's pretty clear.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.720
'x' has to be equal to 'n'.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.680
So the log of 'b' raised to 'n' to the base 'b' will equal 'n'.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:53.460
An example would be log of 11 cube to the base 11 will equal 3.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.800
So these are the three fundamental properties of logarithms,

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.840
and we don't really need to remember these

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.220
as they can be easily derived.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:02.860
Along with the properties,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.900
there are a few conditions we need to know about.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.860
Let's see them on the right side of the board.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.940
The basic form of logarithm is written like this,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.360
where 'a' is the argument and 'b' is the base!

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:16.960
Now for this to be valid,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.780
there are a few conditions about 'a' and 'b' that we need to know.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.660
Okay, first the argument has to be greater than zero.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:28.020
This value here has to be greater than zero.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.580
So log of 0 to the base 'b' is undefined,

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.960
and the log of a negative number to the base 'b' is also incorrect.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.940
But don't get confused here.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.440
The answer of the log can be 0 or negative.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:43.940
For example, as we saw here

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.140
the log of 1 to the base 'b' will be 0.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:48.520
This is correct.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.800
And what will be log of 1 half to the base 2?

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:56.100
It will equal 'negative 1' as '2 raised to negative 1'

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.500
is equal to '1 over two' or 0.5.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.140
This is also correct.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.700
The answer can be 0 or negative.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.120
But the argument has to be greater than 0.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:08.960
What are the conditions for 'b'?

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.820
The base 'b' has to be greater than 0.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.380
And it cannot be equal to 1.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:18.140
Yes, the base 'b' cannot be equal to 1.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.740
So the log of 'a' to the negative number base is incorrect.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:25.300
Why is 1 not allowed as the base?

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.520
I want you to tax your brain a bit

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.120
and come up with an answer.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.680
Why can't the base be equal to 1?

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:39.700
Let's say the base was 1

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.280
and we have a logarithmic expression like

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.120
log of 'a' to the base 1 is equal to 'x'.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:47.860
In the exponential form

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:51.760
it can be written as 1 raised to 'x' is equal to 'a'.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.920
What can be the value of 'x' that gives us 'a'?

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.140
If 'a' is 2, what will 'x' be?

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.520
If 'a' is 5, what will 'x' be equal to?

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.540
We can't really come up with an answer

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.180
because 1 raised to any number will result in a 1!

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.520
Hence this will be undefined.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:13.240
So these were the three simple primary properties of logs,

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.380
and these were the conditions.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.580
With this, we can move ahead and solve interesting examples.

